  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nOutside of the storm risk today, it has been very hot and humid \ntoday. Heat indices have been around 100-105 so far but still have \ntime to increase to about 115 degrees in some spots. The Extreme \nHeat Warning continues until tonight at 9 pm. If thunderstorms \nmaterialize and move in during the afternoon and early evening, it \nwould cool temps/heat indices and would likely end the heat \nheadlines earlier than 9 pm. Beyond today, temperatures become more \nseasonable and less humid with highs in the 80s and lows in the 60s \nthrough next week. \n\nToday's forecast has been a doozy. The models haven't been of the \ngreatest assistance recently with this round of convection. We have \nan Enhanced Risk (level 3 of 5) for areas near and north of a Henry \nto Marshall line and a Slight Risk (level 2 of 5) elsewhere for \ntoday. Primarily this risk is for damaging winds, potentially \nstronger than 70 mph, but there is also a 2% tornado risk for the \narea within the Enhanced Risk. \n\nOur 12z sounding shows MUCAPE over 5500 J/kg, effective shear of ~50 \nknots, and PWATs of nearly 2 inches. A shortwave is pulsing through \nthis afternoon across Illinois and Indiana, based off GFS 500 mb \nvorticity. The storms currently over the Chicago-Land area may clip \neastern IL as it drops south into Indiana and they are evolving into \na damaging wind threat as it transitions to a linear mode rather \nthan supercellular. It is starting to trend towards areas west of I-\n55 may not see any of the severe threat today. There may be some \nshowers/storms that direction but it looks like the main show will \nremain east of I-55 this afternoon/evening. An additional wave may \nmove behind it that will also drop south, potentially riding the \nIL/IN border, but could possibly be weaker in strength, based off \nthe HRRR. The HRRR's 16z and 17z runs are picking up the active \nconvection well. The storms should have exited by 10 pm tonight. \nStorms will be moving to the south-southeast at 30-40 mph this \nafternoon/evening.\n\nThe next chance for showers and thunderstorms comes in on Friday \ninto Saturday as a shortwave moves through the area.",
